\[
Z_t\simeq\operatorname{diag}(I_s,tI_k),\qquad A=(X,Y),
\]
the charge is \(\det(XX^\top+t^2YY^\top)^{-a/2}\). Near \(\operatorname{rank}X=r\), with \(h=b-r\), the normal block has codimension \(h(s-b+h)\), while the determinant contributes \(t^{-ah}\). Thus integrating \(A\) produces the worst exponent
\[
\gamma_s=\max_{(b-s)_+\le h\le b} h(a+b-s-h),
\]
not merely \(a(b-s)_+\). I’m testing this corrected exponent now; this is exactly the kind of hidden degeneration that can turn the answer from bounded into a wall.
